<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n77301" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">πᾰν-δέκτης</span></b> , <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ου</span>,
			ὁ, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>all-receiver</i>: in pl. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">πανδέκται,
					οἱ</span>, name of a
				<i>Universal Dictionary</i> or <u>Encyclopedia</u>,
			such as those compiled by Tiro and Dorotheus, <i><span class="en">
				Gell.
				13.9
			</span></i>. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense3"><b>2.</b> in pl., also,
				<i>the Pandects</i>, i.e. the law-books of Justinian, <i><span class="en">
				<u>Cod.Just.</u>
				1.17.1.12
			</span></i>, al.: sg., <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὁ π.</span>, =
			<u>Digesta</u>, Id.<u>Const.</u>
			
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Δέδωκεν</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					1
				</span></i>
			. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>II.</b> Stoic word
			for 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἐπίρρημα</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					11
				</span></i>
			, Charis.<i><span class="en">
				p.190
			</span></i> K.</div>
